The same structure applies to a classical field. Write all the fields generically as $\phi$. A local field Lagrangian density may depend on the fields and on both their spatial and temporal derivatives: $\mathcal{L}
=\mathcal{L}\!\left(\phi,\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial x^\mu}\right)
=\mathcal{L}(\phi,\partial_\mu\phi).$ Relativity requires $\mathcal{L}$ to transform as a Lorentz scalar. Subject to that requirement, scalar combinations built from fields and their derivatives furnish the possible terms in the Lagrangian.

